 close the wells.”</p>
<p>“We have.”</p>
<p>Anselm tapped several marks with one finger.</p>
<p>“Monasteries are distributing stored water. Carts are bringing barrels from unaffected springs. Priests and brothers are caring for the sick. The king has opened two royal granaries because families who cannot work cannot buy food.”</p>
<p>Another messenger approached and whispered into the Archbishop’s ear.</p>
<p>Anselm nodded.</p>
<p>“Good. Tell both households that the truce stands until the relief roads are clear.”</p>
<p>Alaric glanced at Charles.</p>
<p>“Truce?”</p>
<p>“Two local lords have been quarrelling over a boundary for years,” Charles said quietly. “Apparently neither wishes to explain to the Archbishop why his soldiers prevented water carts from reaching sick children.”</p>
<p>Anselm heard him.</p>
<p>“Nor,” he said, “do they wish to explain it to their own priests.”</p>
<p>Then he returned to the map as if the matter were finished.</p>
<p>Alaric said nothing, but he remembered it.</p>
<p>The Church that could not decide what Vaelor was had, in less than a week, forced two feuding lordships to open roads to one another.</p>
<p>Anselm pointed to an old mark farther west.</p>
<p>“There may be another answer.”</p>
<p>He told them about Saint Armand.</p>
<p>Long before Charles’s kingdom had taken its present shape, Armand had been a knight who abandoned court life to protect pilgrims, travelers and poor villages along dangerous roads. He founded shelters beside wells and springs and taught that a knight who guarded only castles had misunderstood his duty.</p>
<p>After his death, the Church preserved one of the vessels associated with his ministry: a silver chalice used in blessings of communal water.</p>
<p>“The Chalice of Saint Armand,” Anselm said.</p>
<p>Charles frowned.</p>
<p>“I thought it was lost.”</p>
<p>“It was.”</p>
<p>The chalice had once been kept in a remote abbey in the western hills. Generations earlier, a landslide destroyed the road and damaged much of the monastery. The surviving brothers abandoned it. Later attempts to recover the relic failed when the lower halls collapsed further and wild creatures moved into the ruins.</p>
<p>“The old accounts also speak of trials built into the inner sanctuary,” Anselm added. “Not traps for thieves exactly. Lessons. The brothers believed no one should reach Armand’s relic merely by being strong enough to break down a door.”</p>
<p>Alaric raised an eyebrow.</p>
<p>“What kind of lessons?”</p>
<p>“If I knew, I would tell you.”</p>
<p>“Convenient.”</p>
<p>Anselm looked at him.</p>
<p>“You doubt the relic?”</p>
<p>“I doubt anything that waits in a ruined abbey full of monsters and riddles until exactly the moment someone needs it.”</p>
<p>Charles covered a smile.</p>
<p>Anselm did not.</p>
<p>“The Church has used the Chalice before in times when wells became corrupted. Whether you call its power divine, magical or something your scholars have not yet named is less important to me than the fact that it has worked.”</p>
<p>That answer Alaric understood.</p>
<p>“What guards the abbey?”</p>
<p>“No one knows exactly.”</p>
<p>“Better.”</p>
<p>This time Anselm almost smiled.</p>
<p>The Dragon Riders volunteered before Charles could formally ask.</p>
<p>The following morning, Alaric received a small metal badge bearing the Sacred Lily and the seal of Anselm’s office.</p>
<p>“This identifies you as traveling on Church business connected with the relief effort,” said the canon who gave it to him. “Show it at tolls, bridges, monasteries and lordship boundaries. It should prevent unnecessary delays.”</p>
<p>“Should?”</p>
<p>The canon sighed.</p>
<p>“This is Bretonnia.”</p>
<p>For two days the riders traveled west on horseback.</p>
<p>The sickness became visible before they reached the affected villages.</p>
<p>Church carts passed them carrying water barrels. Monks rode with wagons of bread and dried food. At roadside chapels, lists of sick households had been nailed beside the doors. Local people filled buckets from temporary cisterns while priests marked which families had received their share.</p>
<p>The work was not dramatic.</p>
<p>No swords flashed.</p>
<p>No banners flew.</p>
<p>But it was organized.</p>
<p>At one narrow bridge, armed guards stopped a group of villagers trying to cross from the neighboring lordship. Their own road had become impassable, and the shortest route to an unaffected spring crossed the bridge.</p>
<p>The captain shook his head.</p>
<p>“My orders cover Church carts and named messengers.”</p>
<p>Alaric showed Anselm’s badge.</p>
<p>“They are with me.”</p>
<p>The captain studied the villagers.</p>
<p>“They are not named on your authority.”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>“Then the seal does not cover them.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked at the empty barrels on their cart.</p>
<p>“They are going to bring water back to sick families.”</p>
<p>The captain hesitated.</p>
<p>“You are asking me to ignore my orders.”</p>
<p>“I am asking you to understand why the orders exist.”</p>
<p>For several seconds neither man moved.</p>
<p>Then the captain stepped aside.</p>
<p>“Go.”</p>
<p>The villagers hurried across.</p>
<p>Alaric nodded once to the guard and continued riding.</p>
<p>No one spoke of it again.</p>
<p>By late afternoon the ruined abbey appeared above the trees.</p>
<p>Its bell tower had lost half its roof. Ivy covered the outer walls. The old road ended beneath a slope of fallen stone, and the riders had to leave their horses below.</p>
<p>Inside, the air smelled of damp earth.</p>
<p>The upper chapel had collapsed years ago, but part of the cloister remained. Water dripped through cracks in the ceiling. Broken statues watched from alcoves filled with moss.</p>
<p>A stairway led downward.</p>
<p>Someone had scratched an old warning into the stone beside it.</p>
<p>Alaric held up his lantern.</p>
<p>“Well,” he said. “At least this part feels familiar.”</p>
<p>The lower halls had become something between monastery and cave.</p>
<p>Roots pushed through walls. Sections of corridor had fallen away into black gaps. Pale spiders the size of dogs nested between broken pillars. Their webs stretched across doorways and vanished into cracks in the masonry.</p>
<p>The riders advanced with shields close together. In the narrow halls there was no dragon fire, no easy retreat into the sky. Caradoc cut one spider from the wall before it dropped onto an unnamed knight’s shoulders. Gawain kept the group moving while Alaric cleared webbing from an archway with a torch.</p>
<p>At one point Alaric’s boot went through rotten timber and he nearly vanished into the darkness below.</p>
<p>A companion caught his arm.</p>
<p>“Still prefer horses?” the knight asked.</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>Beyond the spider nests, the ruins changed.</p>
<p>The natural cave growth ended at a pair of stone doors carved with the image of Saint Armand kneeling beside a roadside well. Unlike the outer abbey, the doors were almost untouched by damp or roots.</p>
<p>Alaric pushed them open.</p>
<p>Inside waited the first trial.</p>
<p>Three stone basins stood beneath three carvings: a crowned lord, an armored knight, and a barefoot traveler holding an empty cup. A narrow channel of clear spring water trickled from the wall. Beneath the figures was an inscription:</p>
<p>THE STRONG MAY DRINK. THE WEAK MUST DRINK. WHO DRINKS FIRST?</p>
<p>Caradoc pointed toward the crowned figure.</p>
<p>“A king?”</p>
<p>Gawain shook his head.</p>
<p>“Armand left a court to serve travelers.”</p>
<p>Alaric filled a small stone cup from the spring. He considered the three basins, then poured the water into the basin beneath the barefoot traveler.</p>
<p>For a moment nothing happened.</p>
<p>Then stone moved somewhere inside the wall.</p>
<p>The traveler’s basin sank slightly, and a door opened beyond it.</p>
<p>“The guardian drinks last,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>The second passage was a long hall whose floor was made of square stones carved with symbols: towers, swords, lilies, wells, bridges, roads and open hands. At the far end stood another relief of Armand, this time guiding a family through a storm.</p>
<p>The first Rider stepped toward a stone marked with a tower.</p>
<p>“Wait,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>Dust lay thick across most of the floor.</p>
<p>But on several stones, tiny grooves had been worn into the carving centuries ago. They formed a path: road, bridge, well, open hand.</p>
<p>Not castle. Not sword.</p>
<p>Alaric remembered Anselm’s story.</p>
<p>A knight who guarded only castles had misunderstood his duty.</p>
<p>They followed the worn path.</p>
<p>Twice, Caradoc tested a neighboring stone with the end of a spear. The floor shifted beneath it and a row of blunt stone bars slammed down from the wall.</p>
<p>“Good observation,” Caradoc said.</p>
<p>“Good spear,” Alaric replied.</p>
<p>The path brought them into a circular chamber.</p>
<p>Here six gargoyles crouched on stone ledges around the walls.</p>
<p>Unlike the broken statues above, these had no moss upon them.</p>
<p>Their heads turned as the riders entered.</p>
<p>Stone claws scraped against stone.</p>
<p>The knights drew their swords.</p>
<p>The gargoyles dropped from their ledges.</p>
<p>The fight was brutal and strange. Steel rang against stone. One guardian’s wing shattered beneath Gawain’s blow; another drove Caradoc backward with enough force to crack his shield. Alaric struck at joints rather than bodies, breaking the carved limbs where they were thinnest.</p>
<p>When the final gargoyle fell, the chamber became silent.</p>
<p>At its far side stood a low doorway.</p>
<p>Too low to enter upright.</p>
<p>Above it was carved a sword laid across two open hands.</p>
<p>Alaric approached.</p>
<p>As he did, the broken gargoyles began to move again.</p>
<p>Not rising.</p>
<p>Turning their heads.</p>
<p>Watching.</p>
<p>“There is another lesson,” Gawain said.</p>
<p>Alaric looked at the low doorway and then at his sword.</p>
<p>He understood.</p>
<p>He unbuckled the weapon and placed it on the floor.</p>
<p>Then he removed his shield.</p>
<p>The others did the same.</p>
<p>One by one, the stone guardians lowered their heads.</p>
<p>Alaric knelt and passed through the doorway unarmed.</p>
<p>Humility, he thought, was not the same as helplessness. Sometimes it meant knowing when strength was no longer the answer.</p>
<p>The old reliquary lay beneath what had once been the abbey’s well chapel.</p>
<p>The room was small.</p>
<p>That surprised Alaric.</p>
<p>After the size of Notre-Dame du Lys and the ceremony of Bellacourt, he had expected gold, marble and statues.</p>
<p>Instead there was a stone basin, a faded image of Saint Armand giving water to travelers, and a sealed silver chest built into the wall.</p>
<p>Inside lay the chalice.</p>
<p>It was beautiful, but not grand.</p>
<p>Silver, darkened by age. A narrow band of lilies around the rim. On one side, a tiny engraved knight knelt beside a well while villagers filled their cups.</p>
<p>Alaric lifted it carefully.</p>
<p>The trials suddenly made sense.</p>
<p>The relic did not celebrate a battle.</p>
<p>It celebrated service.</p>
<p>When they returned to the western villages, the Church had already prepared everything.</p>
<p>The sick had been separated from crowded houses. Water from suspect wells was no longer being used. Food and clean barrels had been distributed. Priests, monks, healers and villagers waited together beside the largest communal cistern.</p>
<p>Anselm himself had arrived ahead of them.</p>
<p>He took the Chalice from Alaric without triumph.</p>
<p>“Thank you.”</p>
<p>That was all.</p>
<p>The rite was simple.</p>
<p>Prayers were spoken. Water was drawn into the Chalice and poured back into the cistern. The blessing was repeated at the affected wells over the following days.</p>
<p>Whether the power came from saint, spell or something older, the change was unmistakable.</p>
<p>Cloudiness vanished from the water.</p>
<p>The foul smell disappeared.</p>
<p>New cases of sickness began to fall.</p>
<p>Within days, families were drawing water from their wells again.</p>
<p>Alaric watched a little girl fill a cup and drink.</p>
<p>Her mother began to cry.</p>
<p>There were no trumpets.</p>
<p>No tournament crowd.</p>
<p>No one shouted the Archbishop’s name.</p>
<p>The monks simply moved on to the next village.</p>
<p>That evening Alaric found Anselm beside one of the restored wells.</p>
<p>“You were right about one thing,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>The Archbishop looked suspicious.</p>
<p>“Only one?”</p>
<p>“Your Church is more than old books.”</p>
<p>Anselm looked toward the villagers carrying water home.</p>
<p>“And your dragons, perhaps, are more than old fears.”</p>
<p>Alaric turned toward him.</p>
<p>But Anselm had already started walking back toward the chapel.</p>
<p>It was not acceptance.</p>
<p>Not yet.</p>
<p>Still, it was the first crack in the wall between them.</p>
<h1>Chapter 7 – The Feast and the Conspiracy</h1>
<p>The feast was held three nights after the western wells had been declared safe again.</p>
<p>Bellacourt had already begun to sound like itself.</p>
<p>The slow bells of warning had stopped. Market carts returned to their usual routes. The tournament resumed. Banners hung again from windows, and musicians played beneath the arcades near Côte de Lys.</p>
<p>The Chalice of Saint Armand had been carried back to Notre-Dame du Lys under guard.</p>
<p>Archbishop Anselm had not turned its return into a triumph for himself.</p>
<p>That impressed Alaric almost as much as the relic had.</p>
<p>The feast, however, was very much King Charles’s kind of event.</p>
<p>The great hall of Côte de Lys was crowded with nobles, clergy, knights, merchants, foreign envoys and competitors from across the Bretonnian Lands. Long tables ran beneath painted shields. Musicians played from a gallery. Pages carried platters between benches while heralds announced late arrivals with more titles than Alaric thought any one person could possibly need.</p>
<p>Gaston leaned toward him.</p>
<p>“Do not look frightened.”</p>
<p>“I have fought a god.”</p>
<p>“Yes. But have you ever been seated between a bishop and a duchess who dislike one another?”</p>
<p>Alaric looked at the table.</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>“Then tonight is educational.”</p>
<p>The feast was partly celebration and partly politics.</p>
<p>Charles moved from table to table, greeting men who governed their own lands, men who owed him allegiance, men who owed him nothing, and men who smiled as if friendship were merely another weapon kept polished for public occasions.</p>
<p>Yet there was warmth too.</p>
<p>A group of guildmasters from Bellacourt presented a silver basin for the restored western villages. Two abbots argued cheerfully about whose monastery brewed the better ale. Knights who had tried to knock one another unconscious in the lists that morning shared bread that evening.</p>
<p>Near the center of the hall, a minstrel sang the story of Saint Armand.</p>
<p>In the song, Armand crossed a flooded river to bring water to a besieged village.</p>
<p>Alaric glanced at Anselm.</p>
<p>“Did that really happen?”</p>
<p>The Archbishop kept his eyes on the singer.</p>
<p>“Probably not exactly like that.”</p>
<p>“Then why sing it?”</p>
<p>“Because the truth of a life and the details of a song are not always the same thing.”</p>
<p>Alaric considered this.</p>
<p>“That sounds dangerous.”</p>
<p>“It can be.”</p>
<p>Anselm finally looked at him.</p>
<p>“Which is why someone must remember both.”</p>
<p>It was the longest conversation they had ever had without arguing.</p>
<p>Later, Alaric found Lord Raymond standing near one of the open galleries overlooking the inner court.</p>
<p>Raymond was younger than Charles, though not by much, and carried himself with less ceremony. He had spent most of the tournament laughing with knights, arguing with stablemasters and disappearing into places where an heir-presumptive was probably not supposed to be.</p>
<p>“You survived the Church,” Raymond said.</p>
<p>“Barely.”</p>
<p>“And the spiders?”</p>
<p>“Less dangerous.”</p>
<p>Raymond laughed.</p>
<p>“Do not let Anselm hear you say that.”</p>
<p>“He would probably agree.”</p>
<p>Raymond stared at him.</p>
<p>“You really have changed things around here.”</p>
<p>“I recovered a cup.”</p>
<p>“You recovered a cup for a man who asked your dragons to leave the kingdom. That is different.”</p>
<p>Before Alaric could answer, Count Bernier approached them.</p>
<p>Bernier had the smooth manner of a man who never hurried because he expected other people to make room for him.</p>
<p>“Lord Raymond,” he said. “Sir Alaric.”</p>
<p>Alaric still found it strange to hear the Bretonnian title attached to his name.</p>
<p>Bernier nodded toward the hall.</p>
<p>“A successful evening. His Majesty has a talent for making disagreement look like unity.”</p>
<p>Raymond smiled without warmth.</p>
<p>“Sometimes looking like unity is the first step toward becoming it.”</p>
<p>“Or the last step before discovering that it was never there.”</p>
<p>Bernier raised his cup slightly.</p>
<p>“Forgive me. I am becoming philosophical.”</p>
<p>He moved on.</p>
<p>Raymond watched him go.</p>
<p>“He hates Charles’s project,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>“He hates anything that makes another man more important than himself.”</p>
<p>“Those are not the same thing.”</p>
<p>“With Bernier, they often are.”</p>
<p>Alaric had learned in the Valley that danger rarely announced itself with horns.</p>
<p>Sometimes it was a wrong footprint near a camp.</p>
<p>Sometimes a bird that should have been singing was silent.</p>
<p>Sometimes it was a man leaving a feast too early.</p>
<p>Bernier did exactly that.</p>
<p>Alaric noticed because he had spent enough time at Bretonnian ceremonies to understand that leaving before the king’s final toast was almost an announcement in itself.</p>
<p>A few minutes later another man left by a different door.</p>
<p>Then a third.</p>
<p>None spoke to Bernier.</p>
